# necro init writes a starter necro.yaml when none exists
behavior: cli-init-writes-starter-config
args: init
-- necro.yaml --
-- src/main.py --
print("hello")
-- @stdout --
necro: wrote necro.yaml (detected python)

Next steps:
  1. Run necro to see it check your code
  2. Edit necro.yaml: change the starter rule or write your own
  3. Set up the pre-commit hook (see the README)

Docs: https://github.com/WahahaYes/necronomicon/blob/main/docs/writing-rules.md
-- @file necro.yaml --
…
version: 0
# Uncomment to add built-in rules for your language:
# include: [necro:python]
rules:
  no-print:
    command: necro grep --skip-comments '#' '\bprint\(' -- '*.py'
    message: print call in library code
    why: Library code reports through logging, so the caller decides where output goes.
    fix: Log with logging.getLogger(__name__) at the right level.
